1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,835
In a crystal, every electron state
carries a momentum, k, and an energy.

2
00:00:05,105 --> 00:00:07,475
Plot one against the other and you get the

3
00:00:07,495 --> 00:00:09,978
band structure, the picture we usually draw.

4
00:00:10,533 --> 00:00:13,499
But each of those states is
also a quantum wavefunction.

5
00:00:13,769 --> 00:00:18,062
As k moves, the energy changes,
and so does the wavefunction itself.

6
00:00:18,332 --> 00:00:22,452
Here, the arrow on the sphere tracks how
the state is built from two orbitals.

7
00:00:23,017 --> 00:00:27,450
So we can ask: how fast does the wavefunction change,
and how does it twist?

8
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:31,108
The answer is a geometry that
lives in momentum space.

9
00:00:31,378 --> 00:00:32,957
It is called quantum geometry.

10
00:00:33,517 --> 00:00:37,305
All of it is packed into one object,
the quantum geometric tensor.

11
00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:41,962
Its real part is the quantum metric,
which measures distance between states.

12
00:00:42,232 --> 00:00:46,027
Its imaginary part is the Berry curvature,
which measures twisting.

13
00:00:46,583 --> 00:00:50,705
Berry curvature became famous long ago,
through Hall effects and topology.

14
00:00:50,975 --> 00:00:55,588
The quantum metric was largely ignored
until recently, when it turned up

15
00:00:55,608 --> 00:01:00,349
in nonlinear transport,
flat-band superconductivity, and nonlinear optics.

16
00:01:00,917 --> 00:01:03,967
This video follows a review by Gao, Nagaosa

17
00:01:03,987 --> 00:01:06,895
Ni, and Xu, in Reviews of Modern Physics.

18
00:01:07,165 --> 00:01:09,033
Let's build the geometry from scratch.

19
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:14,021
Start with a simple question:
how far apart are two quantum states?

20
00:01:14,291 --> 00:01:16,851
The obvious guess is the
length of their difference.

21
00:01:17,121 --> 00:01:18,020
But that fails.

22
00:01:18,583 --> 00:01:22,208
Multiply a state by a phase,
and nothing physical changes.

23
00:01:22,478 --> 00:01:24,710
Yet the length of the
difference changes a lot.

24
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:27,459
A state is really a ray: a whole circle of

25
00:01:27,479 --> 00:01:29,959
phases that all describe the same physics.

26
00:01:31,317 --> 00:01:34,338
So take a small change of the state,
and split it in two.

27
00:01:34,608 --> 00:01:38,448
One part points along the state itself,
and is just a change of phase.

28
00:01:38,718 --> 00:01:42,403
The other part is perpendicular to it,
and is a genuine change.

29
00:01:42,967 --> 00:01:46,231
The length of the perpendicular
part is the true distance

30
00:01:46,251 --> 00:01:48,363
and it defines the quantum metric, g.

31
00:01:48,633 --> 00:01:51,572
Equivalently,
one minus the squared overlap of two

32
00:01:51,592 --> 00:01:54,650
neighbouring states equals
g times the squared step.

33
00:01:55,217 --> 00:01:56,587
The phase part has meaning too.

34
00:01:56,857 --> 00:01:58,430
It defines the Berry connection.

35
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:01,338
The connection depends
on the choice of phase

36
00:02:01,358 --> 00:02:03,937
but its curl, the Berry curvature, does not.

37
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,795
Both pieces combine into the
quantum geometric tensor

38
00:02:07,815 --> 00:02:12,298
the overlap of derivatives,
with the part along the state projected out.

39
00:02:12,568 --> 00:02:14,805
Its real, symmetric part is the metric.

40
00:02:15,075 --> 00:02:19,948
Its imaginary, antisymmetric part is
minus one half of the Berry curvature.

41
00:02:21,317 --> 00:02:25,977
To see this concretely, take the simplest case:
two orbitals, alpha and beta.

42
00:02:26,247 --> 00:02:28,466
Any state is a superposition of the two

43
00:02:28,486 --> 00:02:31,166
so it is a point on a sphere,
the Bloch sphere.

44
00:02:31,436 --> 00:02:35,113
Pure alpha sits at the north pole,
and pure beta at the south pole.

45
00:02:35,667 --> 00:02:40,260
A two-band Hamiltonian is a vector
h dotted into the Pauli matrices.

46
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:45,054
Each band's state is just the direction of h,
a unit vector n on the sphere.

47
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:50,213
Then the quantum geometric tensor
becomes ordinary geometry on the sphere.

48
00:02:50,483 --> 00:02:54,383
The metric is one quarter of the
squared distance between nearby points.

49
00:02:54,653 --> 00:02:58,313
The Berry curvature is one half of
the solid angle that they sweep out.

50
00:02:58,867 --> 00:03:00,938
Take a trivial atomic insulator first

51
00:03:00,958 --> 00:03:04,385
where the whole conduction band
is made of the alpha orbital.

52
00:03:04,655 --> 00:03:06,626
Every momentum maps to the north pole.

53
00:03:06,896 --> 00:03:10,207
Neighbouring states do not move apart,
so the metric is zero.

54
00:03:10,477 --> 00:03:14,929
A loop of momenta encloses no area,
so the Berry curvature is zero too.

55
00:03:15,484 --> 00:03:16,579
Now invert the bands.

56
00:03:16,849 --> 00:03:20,885
Near the inversion,
the orbital character changes quickly with momentum.

57
00:03:21,155 --> 00:03:25,694
Two nearby momenta land far apart on
the sphere, so the metric is large.

58
00:03:25,964 --> 00:03:29,099
And a small loop in momentum
space sweeps out a large

59
00:03:29,119 --> 00:03:32,493
patch of the sphere,
so the Berry curvature is large too.

60
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:35,686
Now do this for the whole Brillouin zone.

61
00:03:35,956 --> 00:03:38,988
Take a simple lattice model
with a mass parameter, m.

62
00:03:39,258 --> 00:03:43,083
Here is a grid of momenta,
and here is where each one lands on the sphere.

63
00:03:43,650 --> 00:03:45,583
With a large mass the band is trivial.

64
00:03:45,853 --> 00:03:49,061
The image of the grid stays
crumpled near the north pole

65
00:03:49,081 --> 00:03:53,039
the map barely stretches,
and the quantum metric is small everywhere.

66
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:55,720
Now lower the mass through a band inversion.

67
00:03:55,990 --> 00:03:59,647
The grid spreads out,
and wraps the entire sphere exactly once.

68
00:03:59,917 --> 00:04:03,906
The total Berry curvature,
divided by two pi, counts the wrappings.

69
00:04:04,176 --> 00:04:05,375
This is the Chern number.

70
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:09,095
So the Berry curvature,
integrated over the zone

71
00:04:09,115 --> 00:04:11,945
gives topology: a global, quantized number.

72
00:04:12,215 --> 00:04:17,094
The quantum metric, by contrast,
is a local quantity, and it is never negative.

73
00:04:17,650 --> 00:04:21,409
Because the quantum geometric
tensor is positive semidefinite

74
00:04:21,429 --> 00:04:24,506
the metric is bounded from
below by the curvature.

75
00:04:24,776 --> 00:04:27,631
Its trace is at least the
size of the Berry curvature.

76
00:04:27,901 --> 00:04:32,783
So a band with a nonzero Chern number is
forced to have a nonzero quantum metric.

77
00:04:34,150 --> 00:04:37,658
The quantum metric also has a
meaning in quantum information.

78
00:04:37,928 --> 00:04:40,610
Suppose a state depends
on a parameter, lambda

79
00:04:40,630 --> 00:04:43,840
and you want to estimate
lambda by measuring the state.

80
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:48,693
If neighbouring states are almost identical,
no measurement can tell them apart.

81
00:04:48,963 --> 00:04:51,879
If they are far apart,
lambda is easy to read out.

82
00:04:52,149 --> 00:04:57,062
In fact, the quantum Fisher information,
which sets the best possible precision

83
00:04:57,082 --> 00:05:01,495
through the Cramér-Rao bound,
is exactly four times the quantum metric.

84
00:05:02,050 --> 00:05:04,971
This also tells us how to
measure the metric directly.

85
00:05:05,241 --> 00:05:09,052
Prepare a qubit in its ground state,
suddenly kick the parameter

86
00:05:09,072 --> 00:05:13,003
by a small amount, and measure how
likely it is to end up excited.

87
00:05:13,273 --> 00:05:16,676
That probability equals the metric
times the square of the kick.

88
00:05:17,233 --> 00:05:20,438
This is how the quantum
metric was first mapped out

89
00:05:20,458 --> 00:05:24,802
in superconducting qubits,
and in microcavities of exciton polaritons

90
00:05:24,822 --> 00:05:27,648
where it was seen to
blow up at Dirac points.

91
00:05:29,017 --> 00:05:30,603
Now to electrons in solids.

92
00:05:30,873 --> 00:05:34,391
The parameter is the crystal momentum,
k, and each band

93
00:05:34,411 --> 00:05:38,058
carries its own quantum geometry
over the Brillouin zone.

94
00:05:38,617 --> 00:05:42,535
Build a wave packet from one band,
and push it with an electric field.

95
00:05:42,805 --> 00:05:44,958
Its momentum changes at a steady rate.

96
00:05:45,228 --> 00:05:50,558
But its velocity has two parts:
the ordinary group velocity, and an anomalous velocity

97
00:05:50,578 --> 00:05:54,726
perpendicular to the field and
proportional to the Berry curvature.

98
00:05:55,284 --> 00:06:00,299
Add up the anomalous velocity of every
occupied state, and you get the intrinsic

99
00:06:00,319 --> 00:06:05,272
anomalous Hall effect: a Hall current
with no magnetic field and no scattering.

100
00:06:05,542 --> 00:06:10,282
If the band is a filled Chern band,
the Hall conductance is exactly quantized.

101
00:06:10,850 --> 00:06:14,932
This quantum anomalous Hall
effect has been seen in magnetic

102
00:06:14,952 --> 00:06:18,488
topological insulators,
and its fractional cousin in

103
00:06:18,508 --> 00:06:22,591
twisted molybdenum ditelluride
and in rhombohedral graphene.

104
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:24,805
Symmetry decides what survives.

105
00:06:25,075 --> 00:06:29,399
Time reversal makes the curvature odd in k,
while inversion makes it even.

106
00:06:29,669 --> 00:06:32,147
With both symmetries,
it vanishes everywhere.

107
00:06:32,717 --> 00:06:37,115
Break time reversal with magnetism,
and you get the anomalous Hall effect.

108
00:06:37,385 --> 00:06:42,836
Keep time reversal but break inversion,
and the curvature becomes locked to valleys

109
00:06:42,856 --> 00:06:46,923
spins, or layers, giving valley,
spin, and layer Hall effects.

110
00:06:47,484 --> 00:06:52,621
Even when every linear Hall effect vanishes,
a Berry curvature dipole can survive.

111
00:06:52,891 --> 00:06:57,656
The field shifts the Fermi sea,
the shifted sea sees uncompensated curvature

112
00:06:57,676 --> 00:07:01,182
and a Hall current appears at
second order in the field.

113
00:07:01,452 --> 00:07:04,567
This was first seen in
few-layer tungsten ditelluride.

114
00:07:05,933 --> 00:07:09,389
So where does the quantum metric
enter the motion of electrons?

115
00:07:09,659 --> 00:07:10,558
Through the other bands.

116
00:07:10,828 --> 00:07:14,763
An electric field mixes a little of
the neighbouring band into each state.

117
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:18,800
That mixing shifts where the
electron sits inside the unit cell.

118
00:07:19,070 --> 00:07:22,550
This positional shift is a
field-induced Berry connection

119
00:07:22,570 --> 00:07:27,277
equal to the field times a tensor called
the Berry connection polarizability.

120
00:07:27,833 --> 00:07:30,518
For two bands,
the polarizability is just the

121
00:07:30,538 --> 00:07:33,162
quantum metric divided by half the band gap.

122
00:07:33,432 --> 00:07:35,898
The faster the wavefunction changes with k

123
00:07:35,918 --> 00:07:38,620
and the smaller the gap,
the bigger the shift.

124
00:07:39,183 --> 00:07:39,971
Here is the catch.

125
00:07:40,241 --> 00:07:45,080
This geometric motion is usually tiny
compared with ordinary Drude transport

126
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:48,468
where electrons simply
accelerate until they scatter.

127
00:07:48,738 --> 00:07:51,661
To see the metric,
the Drude part has to be suppressed.

128
00:07:52,216 --> 00:07:53,502
There are two ways to do that.

129
00:07:53,772 --> 00:07:58,591
One is to make the band flat,
so that the electrons have no group velocity at all.

130
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:02,762
The other is to go to higher order,
and separate the pieces by

131
00:08:02,782 --> 00:08:06,335
symmetry and by how they scale
with the scattering time, tau.

132
00:08:06,605 --> 00:08:11,763
The nonlinear Drude term grows as tau squared,
the Berry curvature dipole as tau

133
00:08:11,783 --> 00:08:15,646
and the quantum metric dipole
does not depend on tau at all.

134
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,085
Follow the second route, and you reach
the intrinsic nonlinear Hall effect.

135
00:08:21,355 --> 00:08:25,711
Its source is the quantum metric dipole:
an uneven distribution of the

136
00:08:25,731 --> 00:08:30,086
metric around the Fermi surface,
larger on one side than on the other.

137
00:08:30,650 --> 00:08:32,164
Symmetry tells us where to look.

138
00:08:32,434 --> 00:08:35,276
The metric dipole is odd under time reversal

139
00:08:35,296 --> 00:08:39,375
and odd under inversion,
but even under their combination, P T.

140
00:08:39,645 --> 00:08:44,262
The Berry curvature dipole behaves differently:
it is even under time reversal.

141
00:08:44,532 --> 00:08:48,675
So the metric dipole lives in P
T symmetric antiferromagnets.

142
00:08:49,233 --> 00:08:50,119
Here is the picture.

143
00:08:50,389 --> 00:08:54,544
In a P T symmetric antiferromagnet,
the Berry curvature vanishes

144
00:08:54,564 --> 00:08:58,198
at every k, so there is no
anomalous Hall effect at all.

145
00:08:58,767 --> 00:09:02,299
Apply an electric field,
and it induces a Berry curvature

146
00:09:02,319 --> 00:09:05,415
proportional to the metric
dipole times the field.

147
00:09:05,685 --> 00:09:08,488
The driven system now
has a Hall conductivity.

148
00:09:08,758 --> 00:09:13,346
Acting a second time, the field drives
a Hall current that grows as E squared.

149
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:18,033
This was observed in twenty twenty-three,
in even-layer manganese

150
00:09:18,053 --> 00:09:21,994
bismuth telluride,
an antiferromagnetic topological insulator.

151
00:09:22,264 --> 00:09:25,479
Interfacing it with black
phosphorus breaks its threefold

152
00:09:25,499 --> 00:09:28,828
rotation symmetry,
which would otherwise forbid the effect.

153
00:09:29,383 --> 00:09:30,790
The evidence is convincing.

154
00:09:31,060 --> 00:09:35,257
The signal flips sign when the
antiferromagnetic order is reversed

155
00:09:35,277 --> 00:09:40,050
so it is odd under time reversal,
which rules out a Berry curvature dipole.

156
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:42,357
It vanishes above the Néel temperature.

157
00:09:42,627 --> 00:09:44,599
It does not depend on the scattering time.

158
00:09:44,869 --> 00:09:48,064
It is antisymmetric,
as a true Hall response must be.

159
00:09:48,334 --> 00:09:52,129
And its doping dependence follows
the calculated metric dipole.

160
00:09:52,683 --> 00:09:57,441
The effect can even rectify wireless
microwave signals into direct current

161
00:09:57,461 --> 00:10:01,637
and in films of manganese tin it
survives up to room temperature.

162
00:10:01,907 --> 00:10:05,321
One order higher,
a quantum metric quadrupole produces a

163
00:10:05,341 --> 00:10:09,307
third-order Hall effect,
which needs no symmetry breaking at all.

164
00:10:10,667 --> 00:10:12,674
Now the first route: flat bands.

165
00:10:12,944 --> 00:10:16,935
Think about superconductivity in
a band with no dispersion at all.

166
00:10:17,205 --> 00:10:19,843
Conventional theory runs into a paradox.

167
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:25,418
The superfluid stiffness measures how easily
the condensate carries a supercurrent.

168
00:10:25,688 --> 00:10:29,697
It goes as the density of pairs
divided by the effective mass.

169
00:10:29,967 --> 00:10:32,020
In a flat band the mass is infinite

170
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:35,694
so the stiffness should vanish,
and superconductivity with it.

171
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:37,403
The way out is geometry.

172
00:10:37,673 --> 00:10:40,088
Expand the energy of the condensate in the

173
00:10:40,108 --> 00:10:42,929
gradient of its phase,
and a second term appears.

174
00:10:43,199 --> 00:10:46,763
It is set not by the band dispersion,
but by the quantum metric.

175
00:10:47,033 --> 00:10:50,745
The stiffness is a conventional
part plus a geometric part.

176
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:52,583
Picture it in real space.

177
00:10:52,853 --> 00:10:57,691
If the flat band is built from compact,
isolated orbitals, the pairs are stuck.

178
00:10:57,961 --> 00:11:01,011
But a nontrivial quantum
metric forces the Wannier

179
00:11:01,031 --> 00:11:04,265
orbitals to spread out and overlap,
so pairs can hop.

180
00:11:04,535 --> 00:11:08,326
For a Chern band this spread is bounded
from below by the Chern number.

181
00:11:08,596 --> 00:11:11,010
Topology guarantees a minimum stiffness.

182
00:11:11,567 --> 00:11:15,335
Magic-angle twisted bilayer
graphene is the prime test case.

183
00:11:15,605 --> 00:11:20,173
Its electrons move about a thousand
times slower than in ordinary graphene.

184
00:11:20,443 --> 00:11:25,646
Yet the measured superfluid stiffness is
far larger than the conventional estimate

185
00:11:25,666 --> 00:11:29,532
which is consistent with a large
quantum metric contribution.

186
00:11:29,802 --> 00:11:32,644
A fully quantitative account
is still being worked out.

187
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,857
Can we see the quantum geometry
of a crystal directly?

188
00:11:37,127 --> 00:11:38,418
Optics gives one handle.

189
00:11:38,688 --> 00:11:42,518
Integrate the absorption spectrum,
weighted by one over frequency

190
00:11:42,538 --> 00:11:45,835
and you get the quantum metric
integrated over the zone.

191
00:11:46,105 --> 00:11:48,419
This is the Souza Wilkens Martin sum rule.

192
00:11:48,983 --> 00:11:52,469
Since every absorbed photon
must at least cross the gap

193
00:11:52,489 --> 00:11:56,039
the same sum rule puts an
upper bound on the optical gap

194
00:11:56,059 --> 00:11:59,482
set by the electron density
and the integrated metric.

195
00:12:00,050 --> 00:12:04,177
And recently, photoemission has
gone further, mapping a quasi

196
00:12:04,197 --> 00:12:08,120
quantum geometric tensor point
by point in momentum space.

197
00:12:08,390 --> 00:12:11,424
In the kagome metal cobalt tin,
the metric extracted

198
00:12:11,444 --> 00:12:14,243
from the measured bands
agrees well with theory.

199
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:16,224
To sum up.

200
00:12:16,494 --> 00:12:21,223
The quantum geometric tensor packs two
kinds of geometry into one object.

201
00:12:21,783 --> 00:12:27,015
Its imaginary part, the Berry curvature,
drives the anomalous, quantum anomalous

202
00:12:27,035 --> 00:12:31,479
valley, spin and layer Hall effects,
and the Berry curvature dipole.

203
00:12:31,749 --> 00:12:36,203
Its real part, the quantum metric,
sets the size of Wannier orbitals

204
00:12:36,223 --> 00:12:39,163
optical sum rules,
the stiffness of flat-band

205
00:12:39,183 --> 00:12:42,913
superconductors,
and the intrinsic nonlinear Hall effect.

206
00:12:43,483 --> 00:12:44,078
Where next?

207
00:12:44,348 --> 00:12:49,622
Near topological band crossings the geometry diverges,
which could drive strong

208
00:12:49,642 --> 00:12:53,710
nonperturbative high-harmonic
generation even at weak fields.

209
00:12:53,980 --> 00:12:59,320
In fractional Chern insulators,
band geometry guides where these exotic states appear

210
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:03,356
and fluctuations of the metric
show up as chiral graviton modes.

211
00:13:03,917 --> 00:13:08,425
Geometry can also live in real space,
as in skyrmion spin textures.

212
00:13:08,695 --> 00:13:11,757
And it connects to quantum information,
where the quantum

213
00:13:11,777 --> 00:13:14,622
Fisher information becomes
a witness of entanglement.

214
00:13:15,183 --> 00:13:19,200
The quantum metric, especially,
has only just begun to be explored.

215
00:13:19,470 --> 00:13:20,329
Thanks for watching.
